<h1>Week 8 — Extension: A virtual cell needs a perturbation test</h1>

<p class="meta">
  Author: liyibing · Week 8 / Lecture 16 (2026-11-08) · CCBS 2026
</p>

<p class="card">
  <strong>One sentence:</strong> A mechanistic virtual cell should be tested on
  recovery from perturbations, because matching a steady state can leave its
  biological timescales completely undetermined.
</p>

<h2>The point I am adding</h2>

<div id="argument">
  <p>
    Lecture 16 proposes a shared mechanistic language for whole-cell models
    and names parameter unidentifiability as an
    obstacle.<sup><a href="#r1">1</a></sup>
    My extension is a validation rule: before trusting a virtual cell’s
    response to an intervention, test whether the data constrain that
    response. A model can use the right reaction types and reproduce the
    right molecular abundance while getting recovery time badly wrong.
    A common language helps us write models; it does not, by itself,
    tell us which parameter values biology uses.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>Work it</h2>

<div id="calculation">
  <p>
    I constructed a minimal production–removal model at fixed volume:
    <var>dX/dt</var> = α − β<var>X</var>, where α is constant production
    in nM/min and β is a first-order removal constant in
    min<sup>−1</sup>. Its steady state is <var>X*</var> = α/β.
    Model A uses (α, β) = (10, 0.1); model B uses (100, 1).
    Both predict exactly <strong>100 nM</strong>.
    Even a perfectly measured steady-state concentration cannot distinguish
    them: multiplying both rates by the same factor preserves their ratio.
    These are illustrative parameters, not fitted cell measurements.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now imagine an ideal intervention that instantly removes half of
    <var>X</var> without changing either rate. Starting from 50 nM,
    the exact solution is:
  </p>

  <p class="equation">
    <var>X(t)</var> = 100 − 50 exp(−β<var>t</var>).
  </p>

  <p>
    My code gives <strong>69.67 nM for A</strong> and
    <strong>99.66 nM for B</strong> after five minutes.
    Recovering 90% of the initial deficit means reaching 95 nM;
    this takes <var>t</var><sub>90</sub> = ln(10)/β,
    or <strong>23.03 versus 2.30 minutes</strong>.
    One informative time point separates these two candidates.
    More generally, a recovery time course could constrain β,
    and the steady-state ratio would then determine α within this model.
    The useful next datum is therefore a trajectory, rather than another
    copy of the same steady-state measurement.
  </p>
</div>

