<h2>What I would argue against / where this breaks</h2>

<div id="limitations">
  <p>
    I would argue against demanding precise values for every parameter
    before making any prediction. Gutenkunst and colleagues show that
    poorly constrained parameters can still yield tightly constrained
    predictions.<sup><a href="#r2">2</a></sup>
    My example concerns an exact ambiguity under steady-state-only
    observations, not a claim that all uncertain models fail.
    Real interventions may also alter production, dilution, or feedback,
    violating my fixed-rate assumption. A successful recovery test would
    support this particular prediction under the tested conditions;
    it would not establish that an entire virtual cell is correct.
  </p>
</div>

